<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
    Epic <a href="/backlog/3383-a-background-mechanical-dispatcher-replaces-the-interactive/">#3383</a> split the
    conveyor's single <code>runner.mjs</code> process into several long-lived daemons, each spawned by its own
    <code>launchd</code> agent from its own dedicated git checkout. Today every daemon inherits the SAME GitHub
    credential the operator's own shell already has — there is no mechanism giving a read-only watcher a narrower
    credential than a write-capable daemon that posts to PRs. Decision
    <a href="/backlog/3872-decide-the-credential-token-scoping-model-for-the-conveyor-s/">#3872</a> asks: should
    that change, and if so, how? This is an infra/ops credential question, not a web-platform standard, so there
    is no MDN/WHATWG survey here — the prior art is GitHub's own two scoped-credential mechanisms.
</p>

<h2>What today's status quo actually is (confirmed by direct read)</h2>
<p>
    <code>gh auth status</code> on the operator's laptop shows a single OAuth token (<code>gho_…</code>, issued by
    <code>gh auth login</code>'s device flow) with scopes <code>gist</code>, <code>read:org</code>, <code>repo</code>,
    <code>workflow</code> — full read/write across every repo the account (a personal account, <code>chalbert</code>,
    not an organization) can reach, plus the ability to edit GitHub Actions workflow files. Every daemon's own
    <code>gh</code> invocation goes through <code>we:scripts/lib/gh-throttle.mjs</code>'s <code>runGhSync</code>,
    which wraps <code>execFileSync('gh', args, opts)</code> and passes <code>opts</code> straight through
    unchanged — there is no credential-injection point in that wrapper at all. The one representative
    <code>launchd</code> agent inspected, <code>~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.we.review-daemon.plist</code>, sets an
    <code>EnvironmentVariables</code> dict containing only <code>PATH</code> — no per-daemon <code>GH_TOKEN</code>.
    So every daemon (the Dispatcher/<code>we:skills-src/conveyor/runner.mjs</code> supervised by
    <code>we:skills-src/conveyor/supervisor.mjs</code>, the Fix-dispatch daemon
    <code>we:skills-src/conveyor/reconcile-fix-dispatch-daemon.mjs</code>, the Review daemon
    <code>we:skills-src/conveyor/review-daemon.mjs</code>, and every watcher pass-daemon instance resolved through
    <code>we:skills-src/conveyor/daemon-manifest.mjs</code>'s <code>DAEMON_MANIFEST</code>) shares one full-scope
    credential today, unchanged by the daemonization split itself.
</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — GitHub Apps: per-mint scoping, hourly expiry, no native <code>gh</code> CLI support</h2>
<p>
    A <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/authenticating-as-a-github-app-installation">GitHub
    App installation access token</a> is minted on demand from the app's own JWT-signed private key. At mint time,
    the caller may pass a <code>permissions</code> object narrowing the token to any <em>subset</em> of what the
    app was granted at install (never wider), and a <code>repositories</code>/<code>repository_ids</code> array
    (up to 500 repos) narrowing which repos the token can touch. The minted token expires after
    <strong>one hour</strong> — a very small blast radius if it leaks, but it means anything long-lived (a daemon
    running for days) must re-mint on a schedule, not just read a static secret once at startup. One App,
    installed once across a repo set, can therefore serve as the single root credential behind many differently
    narrow per-role tokens minted from it.
</p>
<p>
    The catch: <a href="https://github.com/cli/cli/discussions/5081">the <code>gh</code> CLI itself has no native
    way to authenticate as a GitHub App</a> — it only understands a personal OAuth/PAT-shaped token via
    <code>gh auth login</code> or the <code>GH_TOKEN</code>/<code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> environment variable. Every
    third-party workaround (<code>actions/create-github-app-token</code>, the <code>gh-token</code>/<code>gh-app-auth</code>
    extensions) works the same way: mint the installation token out-of-band (via the app's private key), then feed
    the resulting token string to <code>gh</code> through that same env var. Adopting GitHub Apps here would mean
    adding a minting step (a new dependency, e.g. <code>octokit/auth-app</code>, or a shelled minting CLI) to every
    daemon or to a shared credential-broker script every daemon calls before its own <code>gh</code> work, plus
    safeguarding the app's private key as a new standing secret on the laptop.
</p>

<h2>Finding 2 — fine-grained PATs: scoped at creation, no code change, manual rotation</h2>
<p>
    A <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens">fine-grained
    personal access token</a> is created once, in GitHub's own UI, against a specific repository selection
    ("Only select repositories") and a permission set drawn from 50+ granular resources, each set to
    <code>no access</code> / <code>read</code> / <code>read and write</code>. It must carry an expiration between
    1 and 366 days (GitHub does not allow a non-expiring fine-grained token on a personal account) and is
    auto-revoked if unused for a year. Because <a href="https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_help_environment">the
    <code>gh</code> CLI's own environment manual</a> states that <code>GH_TOKEN</code>/<code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>
    "take precedence over previously stored credentials," giving one daemon's own <code>launchd</code> plist a
    <code>GH_TOKEN</code> entry in the <em>same</em> <code>EnvironmentVariables</code> dict that already carries
    <code>PATH</code> is enough — no new dependency, no minting step, no code change to any daemon file. The
    tradeoff is entirely operational: rotation before the (up to 366-day) expiry is a manual chore the operator
    must remember and re-paste into each plist, and a leaked token stays valid for however long is left on its
    fixed expiry, not one hour.
</p>

<h2>Recommendation (for #3872)</h2>
<p>
    Adopt <strong>fine-grained PATs, one per daemon role</strong> (a read-only PAT for the watcher pass-daemons; a
    write-capable PAT for the Fix-dispatch and Review daemons) as the near-term default: it is the only option
    that needs zero new code and reuses a mechanism (`launchd`'s `EnvironmentVariables` → `gh`'s env-var
    precedence) already present in the daemon plists today. GitHub Apps remain the concrete upgrade path once a
    real trigger appears — a security incident, or the daemon fleet moving off a single operator's laptop onto
    shared/CI infrastructure where a 366-day static secret per role is a materially worse risk than the extra
    engineering cost of per-mint, hour-lived tokens. See the full fork writeup, skeptic pass, and jury charter in
    <a href="/backlog/3872-decide-the-credential-token-scoping-model-for-the-conveyor-s/">#3872</a> itself.
</p>
